# Worktree config — Django + Vue + Docker Compose (dashboard_template family)
#
# Copy to <project>/.context/worktrees/config.env and fill the PROJECT lines.
# Everything else — services, readiness, seeding, slot geometry, the E2E hook —
# lives in django-vue-compose.defaults.env, which worktree.sh loads FIRST
# because of the WT_PROFILE line below. Do not copy those values in: a project
# that inlines them stops receiving improvements to them, which is exactly the
# drift this split ended.
#
# Anything you DO set here wins over the profile. That is the override path:
# state the value plus the reason it differs.
#
# Validated end-to-end 2026-07-25 on a real project (echo_lab_ws): 24.8s to
# create a fully isolated worktree (2 git repos, built image, network, volume,
# db + backend, ~190 migrations), 3.0s to tear down, zero residue verified
# against a global Docker snapshot, and an isolated E2E suite green on the
# worktree's own ports with dev untouched.

# --- load the family defaults ------------------------------------------------
WT_PROFILE="django-vue-compose"

# --- PROJECT: the repos that can participate in a worktree ------------------
# Judgment, not derivation. Exclude research/spike repos and archived ones. A
# mobile app whose Metro/Expo port is set outside compose cannot be offset by
# the slot allocator, so including it yields a worktree that collides with dev
# on that one port — decide deliberately rather than by default.
WT_PARTICIPANTS="backend frontend"

# --- PROJECT: database identity, as LITERALS --------------------------------
# These must be spelled out, not read from the environment. They live in
# backend/.env, which is loaded INSIDE the container and is invisible to the
# shell that runs the readiness probe. A first draft of this profile used
# ${DB_USER:-postgres}, which silently fell back to `postgres` — a role that
# does not exist — so readiness never came, the create timed out after 60s and
# rolled back. It only surfaced when the profile was exercised on a project
# that had never been touched by hand.
#
# Read them from the compose file's POSTGRES_DB / POSTGRES_USER, NOT from the
# project name: 4 of 9 projects in the field break the <project>/<project>_user
# pattern.
WT_DB_USER="CHANGEME_user"
WT_DB_NAME="CHANGEME"

# --- PROJECT: unversioned root-wrapper paths to symlink into the worktree ---
# In this family the root has no .git, so a fresh checkout of `backend/` alone
# lacks the compose file, the Dockerfile context and the gitignored .env.
# Relative paths are mirrored, so `backend/.env` lands at <worktree>/backend/.env.
#
# The worktree ROOT `.env` is reserved for the slot environment worktree.sh
# generates; listing `.env` here is refused.
WT_LINKS="docker-compose.yml dev.sh test-e2e.sh .docker"

# --- PROJECT: paths to COPY rather than symlink -----------------------------
# THE RULE: anything a Docker BUILD step reads must be COPIED. A build context
# cannot follow a symlink that escapes it, so a linked file resolves perfectly on
# the filesystem and is still invisible to BuildKit.
#
# Read the project's Dockerfile and list every gitignored file it touches. Two
# found in the field, both of which cost a failed create before the cause was
# understood, because neither error mentions symlinks:
#
#   backend/poetry.lock — `COPY pyproject.toml poetry.lock ./`. The boilerplate
#     gitignores it, so most forks need it here. Fails with
#     `"/poetry.lock": not found`.
#   backend/.env — only when the Dockerfile runs a Django command at BUILD time
#     (e.g. `RUN python manage.py compilemessages`), because Django refuses to
#     start without SECRET_KEY. Fails with `ImproperlyConfigured` — an error that
#     names Django, not the missing file. Projects whose Dockerfile runs no such
#     command can leave .env in WT_LINKS.
#
# Check per project, do not copy this list:
#   git -C backend check-ignore poetry.lock ; grep -n '^RUN' .docker/backend/Dockerfile
#
# The trade: a COPIED file does not track later edits to the main tree's version.
# That is why linking stays the default for everything a build does not read.
WT_COPIES="backend/poetry.lock"

# --- PROJECT: port bases. Pick a free 4-digit band for THIS project ---------
# PROBE THE MACHINE, do not read this off a table. Real occupancy comes from
# sources that appear in no project file: OS daemons (ControlCenter holds *:7000
# permanently), user apps, and sibling projects' running worktrees. This
# profile's own former default band, 4400-4405, was occupied at the moment it
# was written. `lsof -nP -iTCP -sTCP:LISTEN` at allocation time is the only
# honest check; then record the band here as a reservation.
#
# Two rules, both enforced by worktree.sh at startup rather than left to care:
#   1. WT_PORT_STRIDE must EXCEED the span between the lowest and highest base.
#      Otherwise slot N reuses a port from slot N-1's block. With bases spread
#      across 4400..4610 and a stride of 100, slot 1's DB_PORT landed exactly on
#      dev's BACKEND_PORT — a structural fault that merely looked like a busy
#      slot, because the allocator skipped past it.
#   2. Keep the bases in a NARROW window and let the stride separate the slots.
#      A project whose dev ports are spread across thousands (3700..8720 is
#      real) must NOT use them as bases: pick a dedicated contiguous band
#      instead. The bases themselves are never bound — slots start at 1.
#
# The variable NAMES are not free: worktree.sh writes exactly these into the
# worktree's .env, and the compose file must interpolate the same ones.
WT_PORT_VARS="DB_PORT=CHANGEME0 BACKEND_PORT=CHANGEME1 E2E_BACKEND_PORT=CHANGEME2 DEMO_PORT=CHANGEME3 FRONTEND_PORT=CHANGEME4 E2E_FRONTEND_PORT=CHANGEME5"
